\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Wick states over a ring}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{Let #{A} be a commutative ring in which #{2} is a unit, and let #{M}
be a finite free #{A}-module with a direct-sum decomposition into finite
free summands #{M=M_R\oplus M_U}. Let #{b} be a symmetric #{A}-bilinear
form on #{M} whose form map
##{b^\sharp:M\to M^*,\qquad b^\sharp(v)(w)=b(v,w)}
is an isomorphism. Write #{i_R} and #{i_U} for the inclusions of the
summands. Compression to a summand is that inclusion, followed by
#{b^\sharp}, followed by dual restriction. The eliminated compression
##{c_U=i_U^*\circ b^\sharp\circ i_U:M_U\to M_U^*}
is an isomorphism. The module and both summands stay finite free. The
argument asks for no field structure, no positive form, no measure, and
no faithful pairing on the polynomial algebra, and it is not stated for
a general projective module.}

\p{The polynomial algebra is #{\operatorname{Sym}_A(M^*)}. Its linear
generators are covectors. The covariance pairing on those generators is
##{c(\lambda,\mu)=\lambda\bigl((b^\sharp)^{-1}\mu\bigr).}
The Wick functional #{\omega_b} is the #{A}-linear map
#{\operatorname{Sym}_A(M^*)\to A} with #{\omega_b(1)=1} determined by
Isserlis' pairing rule for #{c}. On a product of an odd number of linear
generators the value is #{0}. On a product of #{2m} linear generators,
not necessarily distinct, sum over the pairings of those #{2m} positions
the product of the #{m} values of #{c}. There are #{(2m-1)!!} such
pairings. The value depends only on #{c}. The same pairing rule is the
one applied to centered real Gaussians in the
[quartic elimination](fdip-0046), which cites
\citet{v2, Theorem 3.1 and Example 3.2, p. 29}{etingof2024mathematical}
for these contractions. Here the rule is the definition of #{\omega_b}.}

\p{Let #{\beta=i_U^*\circ b^\sharp\circ i_R:M_R\to M_U^*}, and let
#{L:M_R\to M_U} be the regression defined by #{c_U\circ L=\beta}. Put
#{B=-L}. For #{x\in M_R} and #{u\in M_U}, put #{z=u-B(x)}. On
#{v=i_R(x)+i_U(u)}, the quadratic form #{v\mapsto b(v,v)} splits as an
eliminated form in #{z} plus a retained form #{s} on #{M_R}. Write
#{s^\sharp:M_R\to M_R^*} for the form map of #{s}. Then
#{s^\sharp} is an isomorphism, and the covariance of the retained
generators is #{(s^\sharp)^{-1}}. Restriction of #{\omega_b} to
#{\operatorname{Sym}_A(M_R^*)} is the Wick functional #{\omega_s} of
#{s^\sharp}. Applying the eliminated Wick functional in the residual
covectors, with retained covectors as coefficients, produces a polynomial
#{E[f\mid M_R]}, and
##{\omega_b(f)=\omega_s(E[f\mid M_R]).}
The moment proof does not integrate the quadratic completion.}

\p{The proof uses a basis of #{M} adapted to #{M_R\oplus M_U}. Let #{Q}
be the Gram matrix of #{b} in that basis, with inverse #{C=Q^{-1}} in
#{M_n(A)}, and write #{A[x]=A[x_1,\ldots,x_n]} for the resulting
presentation of #{\operatorname{Sym}_A(M^*)}. The coordinate functions
are the dual basis, #{c(x_i,x_j)=C_{ij}}, and #{\omega_Q} denotes
#{\omega_b} in these coordinates, so #{\omega_Q(x_i^4)=3C_{ii}^2}.
Order the coordinates as the retained set #{R} and the eliminated set
#{U}. The block #{Q_{UU}} is the matrix of #{c_U}, hence invertible
over #{A}. Define
##{\begin{aligned}
L&=Q_{UU}^{-1}Q_{UR},\\
B&=-L,\\
S&=Q_{RR}-Q_{RU}L.
\end{aligned}}
Symmetry of #{Q} makes #{S} symmetric, and #{S} is the Gram matrix of
#{s^\sharp}. For #{f\in A[x]}, substitute #{x_U=Bx_R+z} and apply the
Wick functional of covariance #{Q_{UU}^{-1}} in the indeterminates #{z},
treating the retained variables as coefficients. Call the result
#{E[f\mid x_R]\in A[x_R]}; this is #{E[f\mid M_R]} in the chosen basis.
The block #{S} is invertible, and #{C_{RR}=S^{-1}}. Restriction of
#{\omega_Q} to #{A[x_R]} is the Wick functional #{\omega_S}. Furthermore
#{E[f\mid x_R]} is a polynomial and
##{\omega_Q(f)=\omega_S(E[f\mid x_R]).}
The identity #{2^{-1}x^TQx=2^{-1}z^TQ_{UU}z+2^{-1}x_R^TSx_R}, with
#{z=x_U-Bx_R}, is the quadratic completion in this basis. The moment
proof does not integrate that quadratic. These coordinate identities are
the module identities above.}

\proof{
\p{Factor the symmetric matrix in retained and eliminated blocks as
##{\begin{aligned}
Q&=\begin{pmatrix}\bbI&Q_{RU}Q_{UU}^{-1}\\0&\bbI\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}S&0\\0&Q_{UU}\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}\bbI&0\\L&\bbI\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
The two outer factors are invertible over #{A}: each is undone by changing
the sign of its off-diagonal block. The hypothesis already makes
#{Q_{UU}} invertible. If #{S} is invertible, the block-diagonal matrix of
#{S^{-1}} and #{Q_{UU}^{-1}} inverts the middle factor, so that factor
and #{Q} are invertible. Conversely, if #{Q} is invertible then the
middle factor #{D} is invertible. If #{DE=\bbI=ED}, the retained block of
#{DE} is #{S} times the retained block of #{E}, and the retained block
of #{ED} is that block times #{S}. Both products are the identity, so
#{S} is invertible. The same reading applies to #{Q_{UU}}. Thus, once
#{Q_{UU}} is invertible, #{Q} is invertible if and only if #{S} is.
The running hypotheses give both, so #{S} is invertible.
The inverse is
##{\begin{aligned}
C&=\begin{pmatrix}\bbI&0\\-L&\bbI\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}S^{-1}&0\\0&Q_{UU}^{-1}\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}\bbI&-Q_{RU}Q_{UU}^{-1}\\0&\bbI\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Reading blocks gives #{C_{RR}=S^{-1}} and
#{C_{UR}=-LS^{-1}}. A polynomial in the retained variables alone is paired
only on retained indices, so its value under #{\omega_Q} uses only
#{C_{RR}}. That is #{\omega_S}.}

\p{The same inverse gives
##{C_{UU}=Q_{UU}^{-1}+LS^{-1}Q_{RU}Q_{UU}^{-1}.}
Symmetry makes #{Q_{RU}Q_{UU}^{-1}=L^T}. For the linear forms
#{z=x_U+Lx_R}, the cross covariance is
##{C(z,x_R)=C_{UR}+LC_{RR}=-LS^{-1}+LS^{-1}=0.}
The unshifted cross blocks are
##{C_{RU}=-S^{-1}L^T,\qquad C_{UR}=-LS^{-1}.}
Expanding the residual in those blocks gives
##{\begin{aligned}
C(z,z)&=C_{UU}+LC_{RR}L^T+LC_{RU}+C_{UR}L^T\\
&=Q_{UU}^{-1}+LS^{-1}L^T+LS^{-1}L^T\\
&\qquad+L(-S^{-1}L^T)+(-LS^{-1})L^T\\
&=Q_{UU}^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
Thus, under #{\omega_Q}, the linear forms #{(x_R,z)} have covariance
#{\operatorname{diag}(S^{-1},Q_{UU}^{-1})}.}

\p{If #{\ell=a^Tx} and #{\ell'=b^Tx}, the pairing rule gives
#{\omega_Q(\ell\ell')=a^TCb}. By multilinearity the same holds for a
product of linear forms: its moment is the Isserlis value of the matrix of
those pairwise covariances. Since #{x_U=z-Lx_R}, one has
#{f(x)=f(x_R,Bx_R+z)} as polynomials. The moment #{\omega_Q(f)} is therefore
the Isserlis moment of #{(x_R,z)} at the block diagonal covariance above.
A matching that joins a retained factor to a residual factor contributes
#{0}. Every surviving matching is a retained matching times a residual
matching, and the sum factors. Averaging the residual variables first, with
coefficients in #{A[x_R]}, is #{E[f\mid x_R]}. The retained Wick functional
of that polynomial is #{\omega_Q(f)}.}

\p{For the quadratic completion expand #{x_U=z+Bx_R=z-Lx_R}. The cross term
produced by #{Q_{UR}} cancels against #{Q_{UU}L}, and the retained square
that remains is #{S}. Each half is legitimate because #{2} is a unit. This
is the same block arithmetic as the [real elimination theorem](fdip-000D),
without positivity and without the factor
#{(2\pi)^{|U|/2}(\det Q_{UU})^{-1/2}}.}
}

\p{The correspondence with formal libraries is partial. Mathlib's
\leanref{Matrix.fromBlocks_eq_of_invertible₂₂} is the block
factorization over a commutative ring, and
\leanref{Matrix.isUnit_fromBlocks_iff_of_invertible₂₂} is the criterion
that, once the eliminated block is invertible, the matrix is a unit if
and only if the Schur complement is. \leanref{Matrix.det_fromBlocks₂₂}
is the determinant identity. No Mathlib or TauCeti declaration supplies
the Isserlis pairing rule or the tower identity over a commutative ring.
TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{Matrix.PosDef.gaussianCondCov} is the real
positive-definite case only, as already recorded for the
[real elimination theorem](fdip-000D).}

\p{On the four-cycle matrix of the chapter,
#{Q_{UU}=\begin{pmatrix}3&-1\\-1&3\end{pmatrix}} has determinant #{8}, and
##{B=\frac18\begin{pmatrix}1&3\\3&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\det S=45/8.}
The product of the determinants is #{\det Q_{UU}\cdot\det S=45=\det Q}.
Take #{f=x_2^2x_3x_0+x_2^3x_1}. Exact rational evaluation of the two Wick
functionals gives #{\omega_Q(f)=\omega_S(E[f\mid x_R])=32/75}. The
conditional expectation lies in #{\mathbb Q[x_0,x_1]}.}

\p{The rule is not the [odd Wick rule](fdip-008K), whose matrix is
alternating and whose value is a Pfaffian. It defines no measure on
#{A^n}, no finite-field character sum, and no Euler product. Characteristic
#{2} is outside the hypothesis; the [half-turn example](fdip-000N) is the
place where an algebra in that characteristic is treated. Nothing here
evaluates the real normalizer or compares these moments with the logarithm
of a determinant.}

\p{Remark. The sum over pair partitions in the statement has no
Pfaffian signs. That unsigned sum is the hafnian of the symmetric
covariance entries. Its odd counterpart is the Pfaffian, the scalar
part of the Clifford product in \ref{fdip-00MJ}.}
